
Dataset: “Toronto Raccoon Activity Index” 
https://open.toronto.ca/dataset/toronto-raccoon-activity-index/
   
 > What software did you use to create your data visualization?

 Excel.

 > Who is your intended audience? 

 Torontonians who are interested in the behaviour of local raccoons, and in the relationship between their confidence levels and their likelihood to compromise bins.
    
 > What information or message are you trying to convey with your visualization? 

 That, while there is a rise in the number of compromised bins in relation to raccoon confidence level, raccoons of all confidence levels have a tendency to compromise
 bins. 
    
 > What aspects of design did you consider when making your visualization? How did you apply them? With what elements of your plots? 

 I was interested in using colours that evoke the appearance of raccoons to increase the visual appeal, and found that this particular style of scatter graph allowed
 for a grayscale colour scheme without compromising the clarity of the information. I also ensured that all numbers and titles are large and easy to read. 
    
 > How did you ensure that your data visualizations are reproducible? If the tool you used to make your data visualization is not reproducible, how will this impact your data visualization? 

 My data visualization utilizes publicly available data, and publicly available Excel features to create the visualization, so it is reproducible.
    
 > How did you ensure that your data visualization is accessible?

 I ensured that I used a colour palette with high contrast and no overly similar colours. The grayscale itself also increases accessibility, for example for viewers
 who may be colourblind. I also made all of the titles bold and in a large font that is easy to read.   
    
 > Who are the individuals and communities who might be impacted by your visualization?  

 Torontonians who have bins that are at risk of being compromised by raccoons. 
    
 > How did you choose which features of your chosen dataset to include or exclude from your visualization? 

 I was initially interested in trying to include more data features in the visualization, but found that the options available via Excel were tailored toward comparing/
 contrasting just two data features. I contemplated also including the "proximity to patio" feature, but so few raccoon incidents occurred in proximity to patios that 
 they did not show up on the graph, so I ultimately went with just raccoon confidence levels and number of compromised bins.
    
 > What ‘underwater labour’ contributed to your final data visualization product?

 Firstly, the labour of the community organizers who originally compiled this data, including making somewhat subjective and likely time-consuming 
 assessments of things like raccoon confidence levels. Secondly, my labour of determining the most important features of this dataset to include in
 the visualization, and the most clear and accessible way to present this information via Excel visualization features.
